+---
+id: dl-004-applymergepatch-non-mutating-rfc-7386-semantics
+type: decision-log
+title: "applyMergePatch: non-mutating RFC 7386 semantics"
+status: pending
+format: 1
+---
+
+<!-- decision-log body. `wingfoil memory add` copies this scaffold, setting only `id`, `title` and
+     `status` (and any `--tags` or `--set` value). Fill the required frontmatter fields and replace
+     these placeholder comments yourself; `wingfoil memory submit` then checks the required fields,
+     moves `status` forward and commits the document as you left it, naming in the commit body the
+     content it carries. -->
+
+## Context
+Some clients send partial updates as JSON Merge Patch; the library must follow RFC 7386.
+
+## Decision
+- The RFC's MergePatch algorithm is implemented as specified: a non-object patch replaces the target
+  (including arrays and `null`); an object patch merges into the target, or into `{}` if the target is
+  not an object; `null` members delete; other members merge recursively. Nulls nested in a patch
+  applied to a missing member are therefore dropped, as in the RFC examples.
+- Like `applyPatch`, the inputs are never mutated and the result shares no structure with them
+  (values are copied with `structuredClone`).
+- Members are set with the shared `setMember`, so `__proto__` is an ordinary own member.
+- `undefined` anywhere in the patch throws `TypeError`, consistent with dl-003.
+
+## Consequences
+A merge patch cannot set a member to `null` (inherent to the format). Cost is O(target + patch) per call.
